 ## Overview
-Ray Marching with Reflections extends distance-field ray marching by iteratively scattering rays specularly upon surface intersection to simulate mirror-like or metallic materials [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard ray marching which terminates at the first hit, this method continues the ray path in the reflected direction, accumulating color contributions weighted by material reflectivity [S4, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from [Approximate Refraction](3072-approximate-refraction.md) by handling surface reflection rather than transmission, and from [Screen-Space Reflections](9698-screen-space-reflections.md) by operating in full 3D volume space rather than restricted screen-space depth buffers [S2, unofficial].
